        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "4f720316-ca68-5e8c-8a84-433d0d124ed5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Clianthus, Clianthus maximus, Callicoma serratifolia\n- Taxonomy: genera, species, Fabaceae\n- Places: New Zealand, Australia\n\nFacts:\n- Clianthus is a genus of flowering plants in the legume family Fabaceae that comprises two species of shrubs native to New Zealand.\n- Clianthus is commonly known as kakabeak and is called \"Kōwhai ngutukākā\" in Māori.\n- Clianthus maximus is one of the two species in the genus Clianthus.\n- The two Clianthus species have striking clusters of red flowers that resemble the beak of the kākā, a New Zealand parrot.\n- Callicoma is a plant genus that contains a single species, Callicoma serratifolia.\n- Callicoma serratifolia is a tall shrub or small tree native to Australia.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9473d26d-ed11-5885-8f81-20fddbf217fd",
        "text": "Page: Clianthus. Sentence: Clianthus, commonly known as kakabeak (\"Kōwhai ngutukākā\" in Māori), is a genus of flowering plants in the legume family Fabaceae, comprising two species of shrubs native to New Zealand.\nPage: Callicoma. Sentence: Callicoma, is a plant genus that contains just one species, Callicoma serratifolia, a tall shrub or small tree which is native to Australia.\nPage: Clianthus maximus. Sentence: It is one of two species of \"Clianthus\" (kaka beak) and both have striking clusters of red flowers which resemble the beak of the kākā, a New Zealand parrot.",
